WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.820
In an adult human, the kidneys filter about 180 litres of plasma a day.

2
00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:13.680
That is about four times the water in the whole body.

3
00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.060
Yet most people pass only one or two litres of urine.

4
00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.040
Where does the rest go?

5
00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:21.900
Back into the blood.

6
00:00:22.833 --> 00:00:28.013
The working unit is the nephron: a filter attached to a long tube.

7
00:00:28.733 --> 00:00:34.393
In mammals, the afferent arteriole carries blood into the glomerulus, a knot of

8
00:00:34.393 --> 00:00:38.133
capillaries. The efferent arteriole carries it out.

9
00:00:38.593 --> 00:00:40.873
Afferent in, efferent out.

10
00:00:41.993 --> 00:00:45.873
Blood pressure pushes plasma through a barrier into Bowman's capsule.

11
00:00:46.533 --> 00:00:52.733
The filtrate holds water and small solutes, but no blood cells, and very little protein.

13
00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:58.827
The filtrate flows on through the proximal tubule, the loop of Henle, the distal

14
00:00:58.827 --> 00:01:00.807
tubule and the collecting duct.

15
00:01:01.787 --> 00:01:08.267
In mammals, the proximal tubule reabsorbs, or takes back into the blood, about 60 to

16
00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:10.727
70% of filtered sodium.

17
00:01:11.447 --> 00:01:17.087
Water follows. It also takes back almost all the filtered glucose and amino acids.

18
00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:22.467
Now the loop of Henle, a hairpin with two lanes.

19
00:01:23.267 --> 00:01:27.507
The thick ascending limb moves sodium chloride out of the tube.

20
00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:30.447
It is nearly impermeable to water.

21
00:01:31.347 --> 00:01:35.987
Salt leaves, water stays, and the tissue outside gets saltier.

22
00:01:36.867 --> 00:01:41.427
The thin descending limb is permeable to water, at least in the rabbit.

23
00:01:42.527 --> 00:01:44.227
Water leaves it by osmosis.

24
00:01:45.187 --> 00:01:50.127
Because the lanes run in opposite directions, a modest difference adds up.

25
00:01:50.867 --> 00:01:55.887
In rat kidney slices, osmolality rises from the cortex to the papilla.

26
00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:00.080
The collecting duct runs through that salty tissue.

27
00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:07.060
With antidiuretic hormone, called ADH, its cells insert stored water channels into

28
00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:08.720
the membrane facing the urine.

29
00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:13.240
Water leaves by osmosis, and the urine becomes concentrated.

30
00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:19.520
Without ADH, the duct stays nearly water-tight, and dilute urine flows out.

31
00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:25.840
A water-deprived adult human can make urine near 1,200 milliosmoles per kilogram.

32
00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.180
Plasma is about 290.

33
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:32.200
Three verbs describe one nephron.

34
00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.640
Filtration pushes fluid out of the blood into the tube.

35
00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.660
Reabsorption moves substances back into the blood.

36
00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:45.080
Secretion moves substances from the blood into the tube.

37
00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:50.900
In mammals, the collecting duct sets the final water and salt content of urine.

38
00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.440
The kidney filters far more than it lets go.
